## Solution

**Setup.** Place the circumscribed circle with radius $R$ centered at the origin. Since $P_1P_3P_5P_7$ is a square, its vertices lie at angles $\theta,\;\theta+90^\circ,\;\theta+180^\circ,\;\theta+270^\circ$. The diagonal of this square is $2R$, so its side is $R\sqrt{2}$ and its area is $2R^2=5$, giving $R^2=\tfrac{5}{2}$.

**The rectangle condition.** A quadrilateral inscribed in a circle is a rectangle if and only if both diagonals are diameters. So $P_2P_6$ and $P_4P_8$ are diameters, meaning $P_2,P_6$ are antipodal and $P_4,P_8$ are antipodal. Write:
$$P_2:\theta+a,\quad P_4:\theta+90^\circ+b,\quad P_6:\theta+180^\circ+a,\quad P_8:\theta+270^\circ+b,$$
with $0<a<90^\circ$, $0<b<90^\circ$.

The two side lengths of the rectangle are $2R\sin\!\tfrac{90^\circ+b-a}{2}$ and $2R\sin\!\tfrac{90^\circ+a-b}{2}$. Setting $u=\tfrac{90^\circ+b-a}{2}$, $v=\tfrac{90^\circ+a-b}{2}$, note $u+v=90^\circ$, so:
$$\text{Area}_{\text{rect}} = 4R^2\sin u\cos u = 2R^2\sin(2u)=2R^2\cos(b-a).$$
Since $2R^2=5$ and the area is $4$: $\;5\cos(b-a)=4$, so $\cos(b-a)=\tfrac{4}{5}$.

**Octagon area.** The consecutive angular gaps are $a,\;90^\circ-a,\;b,\;90^\circ-b,\;a,\;90^\circ-a,\;b,\;90^\circ-b$. Using the cyclic polygon area formula:
$$\text{Area}_{\text{oct}} = \frac{R^2}{2}\sum\sin(\Delta\alpha_i)=R^2\bigl[\sin a+\cos a+\sin b+\cos b\bigr].$$

**Maximization.** Let $s=a+b$, $d=b-a$ with $\cos d=\tfrac45$ (so $\cos\tfrac d2=\sqrt{\tfrac{1+4/5}{2}}=\tfrac{3}{\sqrt{10}}$). Then:
$$\sin a+\cos a+\sin b+\cos b = 2\cos\tfrac d2\Bigl(\sin\tfrac s2+\cos\tfrac s2\Bigr)=\frac{6}{\sqrt{10}}\cdot\sqrt2\,\sin\!\Bigl(\tfrac s2+45^\circ\Bigr)=\frac{6}{\sqrt5}\sin\!\Bigl(\tfrac s2+45^\circ\Bigr).$$
This is maximized when $\tfrac s2+45^\circ=90^\circ$, i.e., $s=90^\circ$, giving a maximum value of $\tfrac{6}{\sqrt5}$.

**Verification of feasibility.** With $a+b=90^\circ$ and $\cos(b-a)=\tfrac45$, we get $d=\arccos\tfrac45\approx36.87^\circ$, so $a\approx26.57^\circ$, $b\approx63.43^\circ$, both in $(0^\circ,90^\circ)$. ✓

**Result.**
$$\text{Area}_{\text{oct}}^{\max}=\frac{5}{2}\cdot\frac{6}{\sqrt5}=\frac{15}{\sqrt5}=3\sqrt5.$$

